<header class="page-hero"><div class="wrap"><nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/blog/">Blog</a><span>/</span><span>Charging watts</span></nav><span class="section-tag">Charging power</span><h1>Mac charging watts explained.</h1><p class="lede">The number printed on the charger is a capability, not a promise that the Mac will draw that amount every second.</p></div></header>

<article class="prose-section"><div class="wrap prose">
  <h2>Three different wattages can all be correct</h2><p>A charging setup has at least three relevant limits: what the charger can offer, what the cable can carry, and what the Mac is prepared to request. The lowest applicable limit constrains the connection.</p><p>Even after negotiation, the Mac controls the moment-to-moment draw. Battery charge level, temperature, system load, Optimized Battery Charging, and the normal taper near full all affect how much power moves into the battery.</p>
  <h2>Negotiated power versus live battery power</h2><p><strong>Negotiated power</strong> describes the power profile agreed between the charger and Mac. <strong>Live battery power</strong> describes what is entering or leaving the battery now. They are related, but they should not be expected to match.</p><p>The Mac may negotiate with a 96W charger while the battery receives far less because the battery is nearly full or the computer is using part of the incoming power.</p>
  <h2>When is a charger actually undersized?</h2><p>A charger is clearly undersized when its advertised capability is well below what the Mac can use for fast charging. A small phone charger connected to a MacBook Pro is one example. That judgement should be based on the charger's capability, not on one slow sample during an overnight charge.</p><p><a href="/features/power/">WhatBattery Pro's charging sessions</a> show how every session ran. On supported 14-inch and 16-inch MacBook Pro models, they also show whether the charger's advertised wattage falls short of the Mac's fast-charge capability.</p>
  <h2>Why power can be negative while connected</h2><p>If the charger cannot cover the computer's current load, the battery may still discharge while the cable is connected. A weak charger, a cable or hub limit, or an intensive workload can all contribute. Seeing live battery watts makes that state explicit.</p>
  <h2>A better way to test a charger</h2><ol><li>Start with the battery below full, so charging is not already tapering.</li><li>Connect the charger directly where practical.</li><li>Check the negotiated charger capability.</li><li>Watch live input and battery power over more than one moment.</li><li>Compare the result with the Mac's supported fast-charge setup.</li></ol>
  <div class="callout"><strong>Remember:</strong> a charger is a ceiling, the Mac chooses the draw, and the battery receives what remains after the system's own load.</div>
</div></article>
